<BaseLayout
	title="Tips for your stay"
	description="Practical advice for guests at Villa Sancti Albani: bug season prep, keeping the house cool in summer, and the small things that make a Maine stay easier."
	noindex={true}
>
	<div class="content content--narrow">
		<p class="small-caps">For confirmed guests</p>
		<h1>Tips for your stay</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			A few things we&apos;ve learned from living here. None of this is mandatory — it just makes a Maine stay smoother. If anything here is unclear when you
			arrive, text the on-call number we sent in your confirmation.
		</p>

		<hr class="divider" />

		<h2>Bug season — be ready</h2>
		<p>
			Central Maine in summer is beautiful, and it is buggy. Ticks, mosquitoes, black flies, and deer flies all do their work here. A little
			preparation makes the difference between a great evening on the deck and an itchy one indoors.
		</p>

		<h3>Bring (or ask us)</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li>
				<strong>DEET</strong> (30% or higher) or <strong>picaridin</strong> insect repellent for skin. Apply before you head out, reapply after swimming or
				heavy sweat.
			</li>
			<li>
				A <strong>Thermacell</strong> portable bug deterrent and refill cartridges. It creates a roughly 15-foot bubble of protection — set it on the
				deck or beside the fire pit and bugs largely leave you alone. There is usually one in the house; bring spare butane and mats if you want
				certainty.
			</li>
			<li><strong>Permethrin-treated</strong> shirts, pants, and socks if you plan to hike. Treatment lasts ~6 washes; safe once dry.</li>
			<li>Long pants tucked into socks for trail walks. It looks goofy. It works.</li>
			<li>Fine-tipped tweezers (kept in the bathroom drawer here too, but a personal pair is handy).</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Tick checks — do them daily</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li>End each day with a full-body check. Pay attention to the hairline, behind ears, neck, armpits, waistband, groin, and behind knees.</li>
			<li>
				If you find an attached tick, grasp it as close to the skin as possible with fine tweezers and pull straight up with steady pressure. No
				match heads, no Vaseline.
			</li>
			<li>
				Save the tick in a sealed bag with the date for ~30 days. If a bullseye rash, flu-like symptoms, or joint pain appear later, you have the tick
				to show a clinician.
			</li>
			<li>
				The closest urgent care and emergency services are listed below under <a href="#emergency">Emergency &amp; medical</a>.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Around the house</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li>Keep screen doors closed. They look closed when they aren&apos;t.</li>
			<li>Check shoes and bags before they come inside, especially after walking through tall grass or brush.</li>
			<li>The Thermacell goes outside only — it is not for indoor use.</li>
		</ul>

		<hr class="divider" />

		<h2>A cool, comfortable stay in summer</h2>
		<p>
			The house cools beautifully if you treat it like a thermal battery. The strategy is simple: <strong>let the cold in at night, hold it in
			during the day</strong>.
		</p>

		<h3>The routine</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li>
				<strong>Evening (sunset onward):</strong> open the windows wide. Maine summer nights typically drop into the 50s or low 60s &mdash; pull that cool air through
				the whole house.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Morning (before it gets hot, usually by 8&ndash;9 AM):</strong> close the windows and <strong>draw the blinds along the rear of
				the house</strong>. The rear face takes the strongest afternoon sun, and the blinds make a measurable difference.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Afternoon:</strong> resist the urge to open up. The interior will stay noticeably cooler than outside if you hold the line.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Evening again:</strong> as soon as the outside drops below the inside, reverse — open windows, lift blinds.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<p>
			If you forget for an afternoon, no harm done; the next overnight cycle will reset the house. The pattern matters more than the perfection.
		</p>

		<hr class="divider" />

		<h2>Practical info</h2>

		<h3>Wi-Fi</h3>
		<p>Network name and password are <strong>posted on the side of the router</strong> (and listed in your confirmation message). The signal is strongest in the great room.</p>

		<h3>Trash &amp; recycling</h3>
		<p>[Add: pickup day and instructions, or hauling policy. E.g. trash to the totes by the barn; pickup Friday morning; recyclables to the labeled bin.]</p>

		<h3>Quiet hours</h3>
		<p>Outdoor noise carries in this valley. Please keep music and voices low between <strong>9 PM and 8 AM</strong>. This is a residential setting, not a venue.</p>

		<h3>Fire pit</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li>Only when there is no state burn ban. Check before you light: <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dacf/mfs/wildfire_danger_report" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maine Forest Service burn report</a>.</li>
			<li>Extinguish completely before bed — water, then stir, then water again. The wind on this ridge can do unexpected things.</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Wood stove</h3>
		<p>
			[Instructions for the stove will live here — damper position, kindling location, where the dry wood is, ash disposal. Add once finalized.]
		</p>

		<h3>Kayaks / canoes / bikes on site</h3>
		<p>[List what is available, where it lives, and any rules. Add once finalized.]</p>

		<h3 id="emergency">Emergency &amp; medical</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li><strong>911</strong> for any emergency. State your address as we have given it to you (the property address) — they know the area.</li>
			<li><strong>Nearest hospital:</strong> [Sebasticook Valley Hospital, Pittsfield] &mdash; ~[X] minutes by car.</li>
			<li><strong>Nearest urgent care:</strong> [Name + address + minutes].</li>
			<li><strong>Nearest pharmacy:</strong> [Name + address + minutes].</li>
			<li><strong>On-call number for the house:</strong> [Owner / operations cell] — text first if it&apos;s not urgent.</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Closest essentials</h3>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li><strong>Grocery:</strong> [Name + town + minutes].</li>
			<li><strong>Hardware:</strong> [Name + town + minutes].</li>
			<li><strong>Gas:</strong> [Name + town + minutes].</li>
		</ul>

		<hr class="divider" />

		<h2>Before you leave</h2>
		<ul class="list-plain">
			<li>Close every window. The afternoon thunderstorm here is real.</li>
			<li>Run the dishwasher if it&apos;s loaded; strip used beds and start the linens in the wash.</li>
			<li>Take perishables; bag trash and place in [location].</li>
			<li>Make sure the fire pit and any candles are fully out.</li>
			<li>Lock the door behind you and set the [thermostat / alarm / etc.] as we instructed.</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
